              },
              "blocks": {
                "text_RMKXdT": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "enable_custom_font": false,
                    "custom_class": "title use-rosarivo",
                    "text": "<h2>Creatures of Fur, Fang, and Wild Instincts</h2>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "normal",
                    "alignment": "center",
                    "type_preset": "h3",
                    "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                    "font_size": "",
                    "line_height": "normal",
                    "letter_spacing": "normal",
                    "case": "none",
                    "wrap": "pretty",
                    "color": "var(--color-foreground)",
                    "background": false,
                    "background_color": "#00000026",
                    "corner_radius": 0,
                    "padding-block-start": 0,
                    "padding-block-end": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end": 0
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                },
                "text_FeWWFF": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "enable_custom_font": false,
                    "custom_class": "c",
                    "text": "<p>Every forest path, windswept hill, or abandoned ruin holds its secrets. Sometimes, those secrets move. The <strong>Beast 5e</strong> category brings these creatures to the surface — not as mere enemies or obstacles, but as part of a living world. These are figures that walk, hunt, and wait with intent, shaped from resin to echo the presence of real creatures in your campaign.</p><p>Whether your players stumble across a den of wolves or a lone predator trailing the party at night, the models in this collection let you shape those moments visually. They don’t just fill squares on a grid. They speak for the wild.</p><p>Each sculpt in this set gives form to <strong>5e Beast</strong>s as they were meant to be seen: focused, alert, and far from passive. The miniatures are ideal for any Dungeon Master looking to bring realism to the wilderness. And for players? These figures support ranger companions, druid transformations, and animal interactions that become far more engaging when the creature has physical presence.</p><p>If your campaign uses <strong>DnD Beast miniatures</strong> to bring tension, character or companionship to the scene, this is the place to find the right fit.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "normal",
                    "alignment": "center",
                    "type_preset": "custom",
                    "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                    "font_size": "",
                    "line_height": "normal",
                    "letter_spacing": "normal",
                    "case": "none",
                    "wrap": "balance",
                    "color": "var(--color-foreground)",
                    "background": false,
                    "background_color": "#00000026",
                    "corner_radius": 0,
                    "padding-block-start": 0,
                    "padding-block-end": 0,
                    "padding-inline-start": 0,
                    "padding-inline-end": 0
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                },
                "text_gJf8K8": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "enable_custom_font": false,
                    "custom_class": "hidden-text-1",
                    "text": "<h2>When Beasts Act, the World Responds</h2><p>Wild creatures react. They defend territory. They stalk prey. They flee from fire or growl at unnatural noise. The models in the <strong>Beast 5e</strong> line are sculpted to show exactly that: animal movement with a purpose.</p><p>That’s what sets this category apart. These aren’t vague shapes on bases. These are defined, familiar silhouettes with muscle, weight, and intent behind them. The large predator mid-step, the wolf crouched before a leap, the elk startled by a sudden sound — all exist here, ready to make the table more alive.</p><p>Dungeon Masters often overlook the emotional impact of realism in wilderness encounters. When you place a beast on the table that resembles something your players have seen or imagined clearly, the scene changes. Dialogue pauses. Players ask, “Is that a threat, or something watching us?”</p><p>These <strong>DnD 5e Beasts</strong> serve many roles:</p><ul><li>companions, when handled with care or class abilities</li><li>wild threats, when used in forest or tundra encounters</li><li>natural guardians of forgotten shrines, ravines or mountain trails</li></ul><p>And the best part? They fit in instantly. Every model in the category is scaled for tabletop use and available in 18 sculpt variants, so whether you want a single stalker or a pack, the board can reflect your vision.</p><h2>Not Every Beast Is Hostile</h2><p>The term “beast” in 5e rules covers a wide range — from the smallest bird to the largest mammal. But rules alone don’t capture how these creatures function in the world. That’s what these miniatures aim to support.</p><p>Sometimes, the party’s druid takes the form of a dire wolf. Sometimes, a ranger forms a bond with a panther or hawk. Sometimes, a party encounters a stubborn ox blocking the road or a wild boar in a clearing. All of these fall under <strong>animals in DnD</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>Beast 5e</strong> category recognizes this range. There’s no single shape or tone here. Some creatures appear to be stalking. Others seem to pause in quiet awareness. The idea is not to assign a role, but to offer figures that could serve many.</p><p>When players see a wolf on the map, but the DM places a calm, seated miniature instead of a snarling one, it changes assumptions. The encounter starts with curiosity, not combat.</p><p>And because every piece in the category is easy to paint, you can personalize the tone further. A warm-brown fur coat, a silver-gray pelt, or storm-black tones — each choice tells its own story.</p><h2>Miniatures That Fit Multiple Campaign Roles</h2><p>Versatility matters. The creatures found in this <strong>Beast 5e</strong> selection can fill multiple gaps in your world without ever feeling out of place.</p><p>Use them to:</p><ul><li>Populate wilderness regions with natural life</li><li>Visualize summoned beasts in combat</li><li>Introduce a consistent “companion” look for druids and rangers</li><li>Represent shapeshifted player forms</li><li>Build random encounter tables that feel grounded</li></ul><p>Unlike humanoid foes or magical monsters, <strong>DnD Beast miniatures</strong> don’t follow complex lore. But that’s the strength — they feel real. Players know how a bear behaves. They’ve watched a bird circle above. They’ve heard stories of wildcats appearing near camps. All of that lives in the miniature before the encounter even begins.</p><p>Every sculpt includes just enough detail to communicate motion and character without overdesign. That gives you freedom. You can turn the same wolf mini into a dire wolf, a loyal companion, or a forest guardian, depending on your paint scheme and context.</p><p>So whether your party is in the grasslands, up a snowy trail, or deep in marshland, these <strong>DnD 5e Beasts</strong> blend right in.</p><h2>Designed for the Table, Built to Endure</h2><p>These miniatures weren’t made for the shelf. They’re 3D printed in high-quality resin, detailed for painting, and strong enough for constant movement during sessions. That means no stress about chips, base wobbles or bent limbs.</p><p>Each <strong>Beast 5e</strong> model is available in 18 variants, giving you full control over its look. Choose based on pose, stance, or even emotion. Do you want a tense, alert beast? A resting companion? Or something mid-leap, locked in motion?</p><p>Because every model comes in tabletop scale, they fit right into your grid — no re-measuring or resizing needed. If your game relies on clearly marked creature sizes, especially when dealing with wild animal threats, this becomes a significant advantage.</p><p>Highlights:</p><ul><li>18 sculpt variants per miniature</li><li>Easy to paint for hobbyists at any level</li><li>Durable resin material for regular gameplay</li><li>Adjustable sizing options available for your table scale</li><li>Base included and stable for use on terrain-heavy setups</li></ul><p>This makes them practical for long-term campaigns, especially if you expect a creature to return again and again — as a ranger’s animal, a Druid’s favored form, or an NPC’s beast ally.</p><h2>Choose the Creature That Matches Your Story</h2><p>In many campaigns, beasts arrive suddenly — a growl in the woods, eyes in the underbrush, tracks beside a trail. But once they’re on the map, they become a lasting part of the session.</p><p>That’s why the <strong>Beast 5e</strong> collection doesn’t offer single-use models. These are designed to support ongoing encounters, character relationships, and thematic story arcs.</p><p>Do you need a wolf that appears again and again, watching the party from a distance? A great cat used by a rival as a mount or enforcer? A familiar that a warlock mistook for something harmless? You can find the right sculpt here.</p><p>More than that, the miniatures support DM improvisation. Maybe a beast was going to be random — but the players bonded with it, or asked questions, or invited it along. Having a painted mini on the table makes it easier to keep that creature around.</p><p>With 4500 miniatures in store and over 12,000 five-star reviews, the wider RPGGuild collection supports nearly every campaign need. But if you’re looking to make wilderness matter, the <strong>5e Beasts</strong> in this category are a strong start.</p><p>Explore them. Pick the variant that fits your world. And next time you say, “You hear something in the brush,” you’ll have more than words to show it.</p>",
